=============================================
6️⃣ Export variable (child processes can use it)
=============================================
export MYVAR=hello
Used in scripts, cron, systemd:

echo $MYVAR

---------------------------------------------------------------
🔹 Validity (lifetime) of an exported variable
---------------------------------------------------------------
Short answer
	An exported variable is valid only for the lifetime of the current shell session and its child processes.

---------------------------------------------------------------
🔹 What “export” actually means
---------------------------------------------------------------

export MYVAR=hello
This does two things:
	1. Creates a shell variable
	2. Marks it to be inherited by child processes

---------------------------------------------------------------
🔹 How long does it live?
---------------------------------------------------------------

✅ Valid while:
	• The current terminal (shell) is open
	• Any process started from this shell is running
❌ Lost when:
	• You close the terminal
	• You log out
	• The shell exits or crashes

---------------------------------------------------------------
🔹 Scope example
---------------------------------------------------------------
export MYVAR=hello
-----------
bash        # start child shell
echo $MYVAR # works ✅
exit
----------
echo $MYVAR # still works ✅ (parent shell)

But:

------------
bash
export MYVAR=hello
exit
------------
echo $MYVAR # ❌ gone (export was in child)

➡️ Variables do not propagate upward.
---------------------------------------------------------------
🔹 Process tree visualization
---------------------------------------------------------------
Terminal
 └── bash (MYVAR=hello)
      ├── curl (inherits MYVAR)
      ├── python (inherits MYVAR)
      └── bash (inherits MYVAR)




---------------------------------------------------------------
🔹 Export vs Persistent variables
---------------------------------------------------------------

Type	Lifetime
Non-exported	Current shell only
Exported	Current shell + children
Persistent (.bashrc)	Every new shell


---------------------------------------------------------------
🔹 Special cases
---------------------------------------------------------------
systemd
	• Export in terminal ❌ NOT visible
	• Must define in unit file or EnvironmentFile
cron
	• Export in terminal ❌ NOT visible
	• Must define in crontab


🧠 Golden rule
	export ≠ permanent
	It only controls inheritance, not persistence.



=============================================
7️⃣ Environment variable for one command
=============================================
MYVAR=hello mycommand


🔹 What this actually does
It sets an environment variable only for that single command execution.
✔ MYVAR exists only while mycommand is running
✔ It does NOT modify your shell environment
✔ After the command finishes → variable is gone


Example:
TOKEN=abc123 curl -H "Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN" https://api.example.com
-----------
DEBUG=true python app.py
=============================================
8️⃣ Make variable persistent
=============================================
For current user
Add to ~/.bashrc or ~/.zshrc:

export MYVAR="value"
Reload:

source ~/.bashrc

Then every new terminal will have it.
=============================================
9️⃣ Read-only variable
=============================================
readonly MYVAR=hello


=============================================
🔟 Delete a variable
=============================================
unset MYVAR

